## 3. P1 — Structural risks

### P1-1　Three independent numbering regimes now drift across shared files

Assuming one `\section` per file (**verify**):

| Shared file | § in `main.tex` | § in `comp2` | Theorem numbers | Equation numbers in `comp2` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `sec2` | 2 | 3 (+1) | 3.x | (3.1)… |
| `sec3` | 3 | 4 (+1) | 4.x | (4.1)… |
| `sec4` | 4 | 5 (+1) | 5.x | (5.1)… |
| `sec5` | 5 | 10 (+5) | 10.x | (10.1)… |
| `sec6` | 6 | 11 (+5) | 11.x | (11.1)… |

`\numberwithin` adds a **third** axis of drift: a hard-coded "(12)" in a shared file is now wrong in `comp2` even where it was right in `main.tex`. Every hard-coded `Section n` / `Theorem n.m` / `(n)` in `sec1–sec6` must become `\label`/`\ref`.

### P1-2　Ordering still non-monotonic

`sec1, secsc, sec2, sec3, sec4, secgr, sec8, sec9, secscale, sec5, sec6, sec7` — `sec8.tex` renders as **Section 7**, `sec5.tex` as **Section 10**. Filenames no longer encode order; rename or maintain an explicit build manifest.

### P1-3　Front-matter layout

Current flow: `\maketitle` → `\tableofcontents` → `\newpage` → `sec1`. If the abstract lives in `sec1.tex`, the PDF reads **title → contents → abstract → introduction**. Conventional order is title → abstract → contents. Consider `\newpage` after `\maketitle`, or move the TOC after the abstract.

### P1-4　Carried over, unresolved

`tabularx` cannot nest inside `longtable` (use `xltabular`); `placeins[section]` vs. breakable tables; `\input{bib}` type unknown (`thebibliography` vs. `\bibliography` ⇒ BibTeX/Biber pass); duplicate `\label`s across 13 merged inputs; ≥2 compile passes; `\T` may clash with future language packages; double-blind check on shared `sec1`.

